La Dolce Vita with Alfa Romeo the most  Italian of car brands the company was  founded in 1910 and has a varied history  this 6c 2500 SS dates back to the middle  of Alfa's hundred year history it's a  car that combines pre-war motor  technology with the aesthetics of the  early post-war era Alfa started  producing the 6c Series in 1927 and the  model was continuously updated until  1953 to keep up with the times  6c stands for Sasa Lindley six cylinders  this 1948 6c already had a two and a  half liter engine and 110 horsepower  right from the word go the engine was  equipped with dual overhead camshafts  but a special cylinder head is only part  of the Alfa Romeo story the kawari  sportivo is as popular today with  Italians as it ever was  walk upload see who's a specialist an  Alfa Romeo says he's been driving and  repairing them for more than 30 years he  grew up with them  his uncles all drove Alfa Romeos and he  was even driven to school in one he  praises them especially as a great Drive  saying they have a fantastic sound are  very fast and have a long tradition this  6c got it's bespoke bodywork from the  touring company in Milan and even took  part in the Mille Miglia today it's  estimated to be worth around half a  million euros  alpha-male enthusiasts helmet lockup  explains that the 1948 model was given  new bodywork after the war although the  construction dates back to before the  war he says these cars were often given  these sublime kinds of bodywork like  this  2500 as as Super Sport with its short  wheelbase only 20 or 23 of them were  ever built  they were the direct forerunner to the  villa destined and Locke camp describes  it as one of Alfa Romeos crown jewels  still today at least where the six  cylinders are concerned although the  bodywork is constructed from aluminum  making it super legit the large alpha  feels heavy and it's steering takes a  bit of getting used to its pre-war  vintage workings mean the driver needs a  sensitive touch when it comes to  switching between its four years  but after you've mastered its  idiosyncrasies few would refuse to take  this car perhaps the most beautiful of  all Alfa Romeos  on the Mille Miglia with its Art Deco  dashboard a nostalgic Flair que bella  machina
 

